    "description": "Consider 3-sunflower-free families F over an n-element ground set (no three members A,B,C with A∩B = A∩C = B∩C). The Naslund–Sawin bound gives |F| ≤ K·√n·(3/2^(2/3))^n. Restrict attention to families that admit a decomposition into a bounded number t of inclusion-chains. Since each chain is automatically sunflower-free and has at most n+1 members, such families have |F| ≤ t·(n+1). Determine, as a function of the exponential regime, the minimum number of chains needed to cover an extremal sunflower-free family, and prove that within the class of families needing only n^(o(1)) chains the polynomial factor improves from √n to n^(1/6). The key insight is that the exponential base 3/2^(2/3) equals 2^(H(1/3)), so the extremal families concentrate on the middle-third layer where chain covers are cheapest. Why now: the exact algebraic and entropy identities for the base are settled, so the remaining uncertainty is purely combinatorial and isolable to the chain-cover parameter.",
